    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d745-a1">

                <pb facs="0708" n="610" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg610"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Lord Salisbury to Mr. White.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Foreign Office</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-04-07"><hi rendition="#italic">April 7,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my note of the 10th
                    ultimo, I have the honor to inform you that I learn from the secretary of state
                    for the colonies that of the 2,000 bags of flour which the Pennsylvania Milling
                    Company consigned by the <hi rendition="#italic">Maria</hi>, 1,959 were landed
                    at Durban; of these 1,759 were sold locally and 200 shipped to Delagoa Bay by
                    the <hi rendition="#italic">Matabele</hi>, 41 having been “short landed.” Of the
                    company’s other consignment of flour carried by the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Maria</hi>, consisting of 1,512 bags, the whole was sold locally at Durban
                    and cleared there. The price of flour at Delagoa Bay on the assumed date,
                    November 2, was £14 to £15 per ton “goods in bond.”</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Salisbury.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
